  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 122 AM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nSaturday really begins an active pattern that looks to take us right \nthrough the end of October. A large upper trough to our west will \ninch closer to the region, increasing southwesterly flow aloft and \nbringing several disturbances within this flow. Showers and storms \nwill become more numerous by Saturday afternoon along and west of \nthe Pecos River. Locally heavy rainfall is likely with this round of \nstorms, but fast storm motion will help limit amounts. Expect a \nweakening trend as storms move east into stronger subsidence with \nonly light amounts (less than 0.25\") across the Permian Basin. \n\nRain chances continue into early next week as the main wave lifts \ninto the Plains, but leaves troughing behind over our region. \nRainfall amounts look fairly light as much of the lift moves away \nfrom our region. Clouds and even a weak cold front Monday/Tuesday \nwill keep temperatures close to if not slightly below normal.",
